WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.620
- Hi, everyone, Sal Khan
here, from Khan Academy.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.500
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom livestream.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.700
For those of y'all who
this maybe the first time

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.647
that your seeing this you're like,

00:00:34.647 --> 00:00:37.300
"What is this link on
YouTube or Facebook?"

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.800
This is our way of keeping
everyone connected.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.130
And when we saw the school
closures start to happen

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.550
it seems like a lifetime ago,

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.390
but maybe seven or eight weeks ago,

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.960
we knew that Khan Academy
had a unique role to play.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.210
We saw our usage go up
by a factor of three.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.330
We started doing parent
webinars, teacher webinars.

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.360
And we said in the time
of social distancing

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.850
it would also be a way for
people to feel connected,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.683
if we did something like this.

00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:02.420
And so this is just a
forum to ask questions,

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:04.430
have interesting guests,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.720
and make sure that all
of you feel supported.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.580
Today we have a really exciting guest,

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.840
I'll introduce her in a second.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.220
But I do wanna give my
standard announcement,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.500
a reminder to everyone,

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.490
that Khan Academy we are a
not-for-profit organization,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:21.400
we exist because of
philanthropic donations.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.090
And so if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:25.140
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.980
I do wanna give a special shout out.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.070
The last several weeks when they realized

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.480
that we were already running
at a deficit before the crisis,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.930
and that deficit was gonna get larger

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:37.110
because of this crisis, our server costs,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.860
our ability to provide
you even more supports,

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.420
they immediately stepped up.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.070
First and foremost, was Bank of America,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:44.150
that very first weekend when we realized

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.870
that the school closures were happening.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:49.990
Followed closely be
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Novartis,

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.890
and Fastly have all stepped
up to support this effort.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.660
So if you, as an individual,

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.030
can help support that is great.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.840
And if you are representing,
or a corporation,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:01:59.673
that is great as well.

00:01:59.673 --> 00:02:02.040
We still need more help.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.670
So with that I'm super excited
to introduce our guest,

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.480
someone that I've known for awhile,

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:08.590
who I consider a friend,

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.510
and, I hope that she
doesn't mind, a mentor,

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.100
someone who I've gotten a lot

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.790
of learning from over the last,

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.300
almost decade, that we've
been working together.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.310
Anne Finucane, who's the vice
chairman of Bank of America.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.130
And maybe a good place to start,

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:25.223
Anne, first of all,

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.670
I'd love to know how you're doing,

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.100
how you're navigating the crisis.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.980
But especially as a
leader of an organization

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.903
that has 210,000 employees,

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:38.850
how is your team coping with that?

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.130
And what are y'all doing to try to support

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.823
those 210,000 folks?

00:02:44.165 --> 00:02:45.640
- Well it's great to be with you, Sal,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.240
and thanks for having me.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.410
So it is busy, it's busy
for everybody of course.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.740
But, at the bank, we are I'd say

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.510
80% of the population
are working from home.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.620
20% are in the office or
in the financial centers

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.900
because we're considered
a critical service,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.060
so we have to be open,
our doors open, trading,

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.200
as well as, being in
the financial centers.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.390
And it's, like everybody else,
a lot going on for everybody.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.260
People are trying to
keep their kids educated,

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.930
so thank you.

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:23.840
While also getting to work
in some way or another,

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.010
whether it's at home or in the office.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:32.010
Of our 208, you said 210, I
think it's 208,000 people.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:34.300
The first thing we wanted them to know

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.570
is that everyone will
be employed for 2020,

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.310
so that there'll be no layoffs

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.670
and that wouldn't be a
concern that they would have.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.080
If you have childcare needs,

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.210
we're trying to help with that.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:50.550
So we are picking up the
cost of trying to help

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.350
with childcare for everybody,

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.800
whether they're at home
or they're in the office.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.260
And then making sure
people's medical needs

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.640
are taken care of.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.600
This is an extraordinary health crisis.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.100
We've never seen anything like it.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.000
I don't think there's
much to fully prepare us.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.690
But as other guests have said,

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:15.690
honestly the financial crisis
was maybe a dress rehearsal,

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.870
9/11 was another sort of
dress rehearsal for this.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.650
And in each of these cases,

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.140
you're able to learn something

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.640
and apply it to the next time.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.330
- And you know given your experience here,

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.750
well you know, y'all have
been stepping up big time.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:35.860
And I mentioned, you know
I'm not just having you here

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.890
because y'all are one of our
big supporters, but you are,

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:41.500
for many, many, years and then
y'all stepped up even more

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:42.333
over this crisis.

00:04:42.333 --> 00:04:44.610
But is a part of a broader strategy

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:46.220
that y'all are trying to do.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:49.020
You know y'all announced a
significant amount of support

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.380
for the COVID crisis,

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.950
and that's all things that
are under your responsibility.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.210
How are you thinking about that?

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.700
Where are you seeing the biggest needs?

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:57.920
Where are you trying to help?

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.370
What problems are you
still trying to figure out?

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.590
- Well the first thing is probably,

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.410
what are we doing for our customers?

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.150
Well like maybe the first
thing is the employees,

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.480
but I tried to give you a sense of that.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.510
For our customers,

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:14.430
those that need it,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:17.173
so a lot of people need
help in a time like this.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:20.520
They've lost their job,

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.270
or they're afraid they're
gonna lose their job,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:24.670
or their partner, or wife, or husband,

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:28.200
or whoever they're living
with is maybe not working.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.690
So we have offered to our customers,

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.940
and they can just go on
the mobile app and do this,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.200
if they need to defer
their mortgage payment,

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:38.700
their small business
loan, their auto loan,

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:42.210
their credit card, they
can do that with a click.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.730
And so we have had more than a million,

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:50.730
like 1.2 million of those
deferrals made already.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.966
And I'm sure that would continue on

00:05:52.966 --> 00:05:54.890
for another couple of months.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.840
So that's one very basic thing.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.770
Another is to work with our customers

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:03.260
on the CARES Act, the new legislation.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.300
So the new legislation has this program

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.980
called the Paycheck Protection
Program for small businesses.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:12.730
And we signed up 400,000 applicants

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.230
for that PPP program, as we call it.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.980
And we have put thousands of people,

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:20.500
that don't normally do
this, against that effort,

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.700
working 24 hours,

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.400
so three, 8 hour shifts,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.310
to try to help people
with signing up for it,

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:29.620
getting a loan.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.270
And just to give you some stats on that,

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:37.460
about 78% of those that are signing up

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.240
are asking for something
less than $100,000.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.290
More than 75% of those
have less than 10 people.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.560
So it's really for the small businesses,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.570
I mean truly small businesses.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:53.030
If you look at less than 100 people,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.770
it's about 98% of the population.

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.640
So between deferring payments

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:00.290
and setting up to signing up

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:04.820
for the Payroll Protection Program,

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:06.700
those are two very significant ways.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:07.890
And then what you were referring

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.380
to is our philanthropic giving.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.720
We give away, or we donate,

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.440
about $250 million a year
in philanthropic giving,

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.770
and we do it at a local level.

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:21.530
So we try to participate,

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.130
through our Market President Group,

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.640
in 90 different markets,

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:30.390
giving to affordable
housing, food insecurity,

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.200
to the homeless, you name it.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.817
But, in this case, we
added another $100 million.

00:07:37.817 --> 00:07:40.470
And the reason we did is because there are

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.510
these emergency needs.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:45.650
And actually, Khan Academy
was one of the emergency needs

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:49.340
where obviously we have been
a supporter for several years,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:52.400
and we have a program with you,
called Better Money Habits,

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.570
that we are crazy about.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:58.350
And we have millions of
people that have used it.

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.730
But in a case like this,

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.840
where you're going to be
reaching out to schools

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:02.750
all over the country,

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:03.930
and helping teachers,

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.480
and those that are
homeschooling their kids,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.750
get up to speed, you
needed money by today.

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:14.750
And so the same as local food
banks, and homeless shelters,

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.780
and some programs helping people

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.170
that are in the emergency medical field.

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:23.710
We felt that we needed
to do that very quickly,.

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:25.730
and a little less paperwork

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:29.793
and a little more haste, if you will.

00:08:30.719 --> 00:08:31.552
- You know what I have to say,

00:08:31.552 --> 00:08:32.385
you know very transparent,

00:08:32.385 --> 00:08:33.290
and I'm not just saying this 'cause

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:34.700
we're on the livestream together,

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.560
but that very first weekend I remember

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:38.260
we had a meeting scheduled

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.770
with some of your team members,

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:41.100
and I was a little late for the meeting.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.057
And I was like, "Oh, I'm so
sorry I'm late for the meeting.

00:08:43.057 --> 00:08:45.257
"You know we're just seeing
a 3x rise in our traffic,

00:08:45.257 --> 00:08:47.080
"and you know all of
this stuff is happening."

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:48.920
And they immediately said,
"Oh, well what's going on?"

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:50.787
And I said, "All our traffic is 3x,

00:08:50.787 --> 00:08:51.700
"we're trying to do all these things."

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.097
And they're like, "Hold
on, we've gotta be able

00:08:53.097 --> 00:08:54.817
"to figure out some way
to help you guys out,

00:08:54.817 --> 00:08:56.331
"'cause this should be kind of."

00:08:56.331 --> 00:08:57.164
- Right.

00:08:57.164 --> 00:08:58.830
- And it was literally over that weekend,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.240
so thank you for that.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.001
And y'all were the first
and then that helped

00:09:02.001 --> 00:09:04.370
I think other corporations
to see the value

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:05.203
of supporting this.

00:09:05.203 --> 00:09:06.428
So super appreciate that.

00:09:06.428 --> 00:09:08.210
I mean there's several really interesting

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:09.140
things you touched on.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:10.100
I am intrigued,

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.760
you know when you mentioned
the mortgage deferral.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:16.166
And that does feel like
the exact right thing to do

00:09:16.166 --> 00:09:18.220
when you kind of lead with your heart.

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:20.270
'Cause people are not gonna
be able to pay their mortgage

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.720
for reasons outside of
their control right now.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:24.600
But what's the conversation
like inside the bank?

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.550
Because on the other side of that,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:29.950
what does it do to the
finances of the bank?

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:33.060
What does it do to kind of
how those mortgages look,

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.690
I guess you know if you view
them as a financial instrument?

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:37.493
How do y'all handle that balance?

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.580
- Well, I think we learned a lot

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.260
out of the financial crisis,

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:47.700
where we had to apply
both humility and tenacity

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.380
to make our way through that.

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:51.700
And as you may recall,

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.220
there were a lot of foreclosures.

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:57.560
There was also a glut of
housing on the market.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.280
Today it's very different.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.040
First of all, this is a health emergency,

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.990
not a financial crisis.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:08.940
Although the finances of the
industry and of the country

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.060
are certainly gonna suffer here.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:13.230
So the thinking around this was

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.370
let's see if we can help
people to defer payments

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:17.280
for a period of time,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.900
while the country tries
to get back on it's feet.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.330
And with the hope that through

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:24.100
these Paycheck Protection Programs,

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:25.380
through our deferrals,

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:29.650
through all that is
being done legislatively

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.450
on a bipartisan basis,

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:34.890
that all of that collectively,
and most importantly,

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.400
the kinds of things that Bill Gates

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.340
was talking about the other day,

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:39.470
in terms of coming up

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:43.490
with vaccinations and continuing

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:47.380
the physical distancing
programs until we have that,

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.180
All of that is to prepare

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:51.660
for getting ourselves back in shape.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.580
And I have to say that...

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:59.770
I talked to our research
analysts yesterday.

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:04.110
And what they are proposing
we're looking at is

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:08.700
we've already had an
almost 5% downward turn

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:10.730
on our GDP for the first quarter,

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:13.620
and that quarter was okay until March.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:16.390
It's gonna be quite extreme
in the second quarter.

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:19.050
But the third quarter looks
like it's gonna stabilize.

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:22.070
And then we're gonna see a
move up in the fourth quarter.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.963
If we can apply all that
we're talking about,

00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:30.410
the deferrals, the Paycheck Program,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:34.520
the kinds of activity
that the Fed and Treasury

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.270
are trying to do to keep
liquidity in the system,

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:40.650
that those companies that are
now sort of on the sidelines

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:42.150
can come back into business.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:45.090
And that may be well into the summer,

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:46.500
but you know we're hopeful.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.600
And once that happens,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.360
the fundamentals of the economy

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.210
should in large part come back.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:58.290
It doesn't mean that restaurants
that we all know and love

00:11:58.290 --> 00:11:59.670
aren't gonna go out of business.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:02.230
It doesn't mean that some small businesses

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:03.490
will have great difficulty.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:05.680
But it does mean, on balance,

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:06.990
that we are more hopeful.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.300
Still we see, in aggregate, probably

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.040
about sort of the ups and downs

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:13.873
I just talked about,

00:12:13.873 --> 00:12:18.070
maybe a 6% downward trend for 2020,

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.533
and probably about the same up for 2021.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:23.710
- Oh that's super useful.

