00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:13.124
So while we wouldn't normally advise that,

00:17:13.124 --> 00:17:17.283
that is cash that's available
to be used, if you need it.

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:20.440
How did I become me?

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:25.334
Well so I am not really a banker actually.

00:17:25.334 --> 00:17:30.334
I understand banking but my
background was in city politics,

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.260
and then in broadcasting,

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:35.290
to advertising, marketing, and research.

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:38.850
And I came to the bank about 25 years ago,

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:40.290
actually a predecessor bank,

00:17:40.290 --> 00:17:43.840
or an acquired bank of Bank of America.

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:47.610
And I was brought in because
they needed some help

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.780
given that how many
mergers and acquisitions

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:53.070
were going on in the
financial services industry.

00:17:53.070 --> 00:17:55.760
And I probably understood
the outside world

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.300
better than I understood
the inside of a bank.

00:17:58.300 --> 00:18:02.820
So I did that work for Fleet Bank,

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.840
which was a regional bank for New England,

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:08.000
and then later for the Northeast.

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.620
Bank of America acquired us in 2004.

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:14.660
And honestly, I didn't think
I would stay with the bank.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:18.410
They were headquartered in
Charlotte, North Carolina,

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:19.943
I'm from Boston.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:22.890
I spend most of my time somewhere

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.750
between Washington, New York, and Boston.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.624
So I had maybe been to
Charlotte once or twice,

00:18:28.624 --> 00:18:31.400
and mostly just to change planes,

00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:35.650
you know it's a big
connecting flight location.

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:37.270
So I didn't think it would work out

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:38.290
but it did work out.

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:42.490
And it worked out because
in their acquisition

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:45.129
of our company there were questions

00:18:45.129 --> 00:18:47.250
that were being raised in Washington,

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:49.690
by the head of the
Financial Services Committee

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:51.870
in the House, that was Barney Frank,

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:56.570
and by the head of the Senate
Banking Committee, Chris Dodd.

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:59.080
Luckily for us that they
were both from New England.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:01.160
I understood where their issues were.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:04.000
And I think that we
were able to ameliorate

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.263
the kinds of concerns
they had about employment,

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:11.920
banking practices, et cetera.

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:15.010
And I got to know this company
better, Bank of America,

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:17.650
and I was very impressed with
the work they were doing.

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:22.460
And that allowed me to maybe
show them what I could do.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.020
And certainly I was impressed
with what the bank was doing.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.240
And so we went from there.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:29.460
But as far as the past ones,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:33.770
I think everything's kind of
a prologue for your future.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:34.717
You take each thing you have

00:19:34.717 --> 00:19:37.110
and you try to apply it to the next thing.

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:39.260
I didn't understand
banking particularly well.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:43.980
Bankers had been my clients but
I hadn't been inside a bank.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:47.820
Actually I'd never been
inside a large public company,

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:51.310
other than a brief time for a company

00:19:51.310 --> 00:19:54.160
that then dissolved later.

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:58.670
So you know you just one
thing begets another,

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:00.660
you apply what you've
learned from the past

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.440
and you stay where you're happy.

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:07.110
So in each of these
cases, I've been happy.

00:20:07.110 --> 00:20:09.310
The bank was probably
not something I thought

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:11.310
that they would either
particularly like me

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:14.380
or that I could see myself
in a big corporation.

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:17.110
But the kinds of things we
just talked about earlier,

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:20.200
the ability to give what
will now be $350 million

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:24.720
to worthy causes and actually help.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:27.850
Whether it's food security,
or helping with education,

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:32.390
or affordable housing,
or environmental causes,

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:34.450
that's really important stuff

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:37.590
and it takes big companies
to be able to do that.

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:39.570
It takes small operations as well.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.750
So when you can combine,
that's really important.

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:45.490
And the trick is not to overwhelm.

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.140
So in our case, with Khan Academy,

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:50.640
I'll tell you, and I've told
you this before over dinner,

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:53.730
that the way we came to Khan Academy is

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:57.120
I was working with my daughter
who had a learning disability

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:59.760
and math was particularly
difficult for her.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.393
So I discovered you,

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:05.380
and we started to use

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:09.240
Khan Academy for her to
relearn some math skills

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:13.020
that she was getting rusty on.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:15.107
And it was so good that I thought,

00:21:15.107 --> 00:21:17.447
"Well, we're supposed to
do financial literacy,

00:21:17.447 --> 00:21:19.987
"and some of what we've
got is kind of boring,

00:21:19.987 --> 00:21:21.630
"and I wouldn't watch it myself."

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.330
Which is always a pretty good bellwether

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:24.500
for whether it's good or not,

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:26.530
would you watch something yourself?

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:28.827
And I'd been watching what
you did and I thought,

00:21:28.827 --> 00:21:32.697
"Could we get you to come in and help us

00:21:32.697 --> 00:21:34.500
"figure out how to talk to people?"

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:37.680
And the first video you did for us was

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.710
The Anatomy of a Paycheck.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:43.820
And it was very, very successful

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:45.890
because who doesn't wanna know,

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:48.580
your first job let's just say,

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.740
you think you're going
to be making $500 a week.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:54.870
And you look at your check and it's $275,

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:56.090
what happened to my money?

00:21:56.090 --> 00:21:59.480
So the ability to take people through

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:02.250
what each of those deductions are,

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.100
and what they mean,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:05.740
and whether you'll ever see it again,

00:22:05.740 --> 00:22:07.410
I thought was so terrific.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:09.970
And we had millions of views of that.

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:11.890
So if I thought it was gonna work,

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:14.693
that first video told
us it was a home run.

00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:17.220
- No, well thanks for that.

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:18.930
And you know I think your journey,

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:20.800
I wanna maybe double
click on it a little bit,

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:22.820
and it'll maybe make you
feel a little bit awkward

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:24.670
'cause it's sometimes tough
talking about yourself

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:26.180
or your own strengths.

00:22:26.180 --> 00:22:28.020
But clearly there's something,

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:29.237
you know you've kind of said,

00:22:29.237 --> 00:22:30.667
"Well you know I was a bit of an outsider.

00:22:30.667 --> 00:22:32.337
"I came to a bank 25 years ago,

00:22:32.337 --> 00:22:34.267
"and you know good things happened.

00:22:34.267 --> 00:22:35.487
"You know I like it here

00:22:35.487 --> 00:22:37.377
"and now I'm vice chair of the bank."

00:22:40.580 --> 00:22:42.480
What traits did you bring to the table,

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:44.940
that actually maybe aren't even as obvious

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:47.260
or as intuitive for
young people out there,

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:49.800
that allowed you to thrive?

00:22:49.800 --> 00:22:51.530
What people saw in you that,

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:52.920
were they always you know,

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:54.780
when there was an opportunity
for more leadership,

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:56.087
they essentially said,

00:22:56.087 --> 00:22:59.007
"Hey, we think that's a
job for Anne Finucane"?

00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:02.740
- Yeah, actually I don't
think it went that way.

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:06.070
I'm usually not like the immediate thought

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.530
because I am little bit of an outsider.

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:11.920
But I'm a lifelong learner,

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:16.130
I am curious, I enjoy other people.

00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:20.060
So I'm perfectly willing
to absorb your knowledge

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:23.320
and then see if I can apply mine to it,

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:24.940
and see if we can get to something better.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:27.930
So I think that makes a difference.

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.750
And over time I don't like crisis,

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:32.750
and we've had plenty of them.

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:34.440
So in mergers and acquisitions

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.060
that sort of feels like
a very acute moment,

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:41.053
and that probably is for
me when I'm at my best.

00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:47.300
So maybe that means I'm
a little flaky otherwise,

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:50.890
but I like being able

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:54.460
to apply lots of thinking,

00:23:54.460 --> 00:23:57.220
lots of reading, I read all the time.

00:23:57.220 --> 00:23:59.770
The experiences that I've had and others,

00:23:59.770 --> 00:24:01.080
try to aggregate it.

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:03.920
Take complicated things and simplify them

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:05.460
and let's see if we can make them better.

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:06.460
And like I said,

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:08.730
I enjoy working on a team.

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:11.360
And I believe in creativity,

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.340
and I believe creativity
doesn't come from one source.

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:18.140
I think it comes in all different manners

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:19.640
and from all different people.

00:24:21.143 --> 00:24:22.907
- Yeah, yep, and I mean well I think

00:24:22.907 --> 00:24:24.260
one of the interesting things you said,

00:24:24.260 --> 00:24:26.040
which I think it definitely resonates,

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:28.150
is in a time of crisis I
think people are realizing,

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:29.419
and that's when you get to see people

00:24:29.419 --> 00:24:30.930
and who they really are,

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:33.770
and I think people have
probably seen something in you,

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:36.113
your ability to handle that well.

00:24:37.115 --> 00:24:38.170
And I mean related to that,

00:24:38.170 --> 00:24:39.980
this is actually J.R. Foster is asking

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:41.407
a lot of good questions from YouTube,

00:24:41.407 --> 00:24:43.157
"What are some leadership skills

00:24:43.157 --> 00:24:44.757
"that you've developed over the years,

00:24:44.757 --> 00:24:46.177
"that you know they might not teach

00:24:46.177 --> 00:24:47.877
"in business school, so to speak?"

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:53.910
- Read, I think that's a
very underrated skill, read.

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:56.870
And it turns out a lot
of people don't read.

00:24:56.870 --> 00:24:59.580
And then read broadly.

00:24:59.580 --> 00:25:03.173
I mean read the news of
the day, read literature.

00:25:05.460 --> 00:25:08.840
Try to pursue what you may
not be particularly good at.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:12.080
I'm not very good at science
but I think it's interesting,

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:16.060
and I read about science
and medical issues.

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:21.060
So it's the ability to have
the humility to read and learn

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:23.020
what you're not particularly good at,

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:25.360
and combine it with what you like.

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:28.623
So I think that's huge, reading.

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:35.560
Listening, I think
listening is way underrated.

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:37.480
And as it turns out,

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:38.930
the further up the food chain you go

00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:41.203
the less people seem to listen.

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:44.210
So if you're not the one always talking,

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:46.100
and you're listening,
and you've been reading,

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:47.887
and you can aggregate
a lot of information,

00:25:47.887 --> 00:25:49.690
and you can simplify it,

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:52.800
those are leadership skills I
don't think anybody teaches,

00:25:52.800 --> 00:25:55.513
but for me, they've been
what's gotten me through.

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:58.949
- That's very powerful.

00:25:58.949 --> 00:26:01.480
On the equity side of
things, from Facebook,

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:04.387
we have this question
from Kristine Xu asking,

00:26:04.387 --> 00:26:06.157
"How are you, and the
team at Bank of America,

00:26:06.157 --> 00:26:08.097
"thinking about issues
of equity in regards

00:26:08.097 --> 00:26:10.207
"to access to capital that have been

00:26:10.207 --> 00:26:12.057
"potentially exacerbated by this crisis?

00:26:12.057 --> 00:26:13.357
"Thank you for your time."

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:21.422
- Well, I think this Paycheck Program

00:26:21.422 --> 00:26:23.803
is a pretty good example of it.

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:29.817
If 98% of the people that can
get this small business loan

00:26:29.817 --> 00:26:34.260
are small businesses that
have under 100 people,

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:38.430
that is an important step

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:40.540
to making sure you're
repairing the country,

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:43.390
because that is what America looks like,

00:26:43.390 --> 00:26:45.710
small businesses with under 100 people.

00:26:45.710 --> 00:26:47.098
So I think it's important that,

00:26:47.098 --> 00:26:48.770
on this program by the way,

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:52.813
that the money runs out
when the need runs out.

00:26:54.185 --> 00:26:56.590
And we've had two triages,

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:59.540
the first one was for $350 billion,

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:01.800
that money ran out in two weeks.

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:04.890
This new one that went online yesterday,

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:08.800
at $310 billion, is gonna run out.

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:13.800
And I think that Congress
has to reallocate more money

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.490
until everybody has been served

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:18.280
and we can get businesses,

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:21.860
so the idea is that you
are able to pay people

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:24.210
the next 2 1/2 to three months,

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:27.190
while you may not be able to
actually run your businesses.

00:27:27.190 --> 00:27:31.150
So when that point comes
when you can go back to work,

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.350
or reopen your business,

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:36.270
the individuals that
have been working for you

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:37.720
have been paid along the way.

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:40.800
So it's 75% a paycheck,

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:44.810
and like 25% is rent or mortgage,

00:27:44.810 --> 00:27:46.610
or the kinds of things, the overhead,

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:48.700
that you would have for that.

00:27:48.700 --> 00:27:50.550
That's one way of thinking of it.

00:27:50.550 --> 00:27:55.550
The other is to always try
to be fair in your fees,

00:27:57.143 --> 00:27:59.620
to try to have programs
like Better Money Habits,

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:02.353
where you're literally
trying to educate people

00:28:02.353 --> 00:28:06.483
on how to improve their
FICO score, rent versus buy,

00:28:10.690 --> 00:28:14.020
what kind of mortgages
might be available to people

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:17.400
versus for first-time buyers, et cetera.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:21.960
So you know we are the largest
consumer bank in the country.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:24.170
We're the second largest bank,

00:28:24.170 --> 00:28:26.490
but the largest in terms
of we're the largest

00:28:26.490 --> 00:28:29.080
small business lender, for instance.

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:33.510
We have a very big portfolio
in the LMI community,

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:37.168
meaning the low-to-moderate
income communities.

00:28:37.168 --> 00:28:41.820
And that's evaluated by the
government and everyone else,

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:44.580
so we have an outstanding rating

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:46.310
from the government on that.

00:28:46.310 --> 00:28:49.310
And it's being good at those programs.

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:52.040
I think we were very good
at them for many years.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:53.580
I think the financial crisis

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:55.540
was not only a low point for the country,

00:28:55.540 --> 00:28:58.220
but a low point for Bank of America.

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:00.130
I think we took that in,

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:02.690
I think we were chastened by it,

00:29:02.690 --> 00:29:05.170
we made many changes in the bank.

00:29:05.170 --> 00:29:07.810
Whether it's from the
really prosaic things

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:10.400
like looking at fees and services,

00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:14.083
to what we were to offer in
terms of lines of business,

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:18.440
not having overdrafts...

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:21.930
At point-of-sale.

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:26.230
So just trying to do
everything you can think of.

00:29:26.230 --> 00:29:28.970
Like laying out everything
you do as a company

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:30.780
and saying, "Is this fair?"

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:33.100
So we still need to make money.

00:29:33.100 --> 00:29:34.390
Credit cards are not free,

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:36.820
there are interest
rates that go with them.

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:39.410
If you write a bunch of checks

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:43.300
and they're you know all overdrafts,

00:29:43.300 --> 00:29:44.713
you will pay for that.

00:29:46.330 --> 00:29:48.410
If you don't have the money to pay

00:29:48.410 --> 00:29:50.060
for something at a point-of-sale,

00:29:51.419 --> 00:29:53.380
you know it won't be able to be bought

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:56.170
because you don't wanna
take that overdraft charge.

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.630
You don't wanna keep running up bills

00:29:58.630 --> 00:30:00.297
that you can't afford to pay.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.593
- Right, and I think it's an important--

00:30:03.593 --> 00:30:07.110
- So those are some of the
things that we're doing.

00:30:08.150 --> 00:30:09.470
- Yeah, no, no.
- Sorry.

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:10.303
- That's incredible.

00:30:10.303 --> 00:30:11.940
And no, no, I mean it's amazing

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:13.190
how many questions are coming through.

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:14.940
And whenever we're having
a good conversation

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:16.962
the time seems to go incredibly fast.

00:30:16.962 --> 00:30:18.120
If you have a couple of minutes,

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:19.340
I mean there's a lot of questions.

00:30:19.340 --> 00:30:21.650
People are obviously curious
about the economy itself,

00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:23.090
and you have a very unique vantage point.

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.017
And you have from
Facebook, Marta Wyngaard,

00:30:25.017 --> 00:30:27.040
"Is this a crazy time to buy a house?"

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:28.407
Krish Shah, from YouTube,

00:30:28.407 --> 00:30:29.647
"Should we be investing in the market,

00:30:29.647 --> 00:30:30.960
"since the markets are down?"

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:34.507
Morakinyo Olugbile,

00:30:34.507 --> 00:30:36.290
"Is this a good time to
refinance a mortgage?"

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:38.500
So that all goes into
this category of you know

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:40.670
people are just you
know should you freeze,

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:44.280
should you act, should you just
wait for things to play out?

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:46.360
What are the scenarios of how
this economy might play out?

00:30:46.360 --> 00:30:47.530
You talked about it a little bit.

00:30:47.530 --> 00:30:49.993
Sounds like you're expecting
hopefully some normalization

00:30:49.993 --> 00:30:51.653
in the back half of this year.

00:30:53.106 --> 00:30:55.610
- Yeah, fourth quarter
is probably the beginning

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:57.080
of the normalization.

00:30:57.080 --> 00:30:58.723
So you know first of all,

00:30:59.890 --> 00:31:03.360
because it's a health crisis
it's very hard to predict

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.070
when the states will open,

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:07.190
when the cities will open,

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:09.290
and therefore, when the
businesses will open,

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:11.640
so we're kind of following along.

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:14.250
But presuming that, we're looking at

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:18.400
at least some of business opening,

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:20.600
and I'm talking about small businesses,

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:21.950
opening in the summer

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:24.493
and perhaps schools opening in September.

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:29.150
We do see normalization, of some sort,

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:29.983
in the fourth quarter.

00:31:29.983 --> 00:31:34.130
And then, as I said, in 2021
we see things improving.

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:38.210
So overall down in 2020 at about 6%,

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:41.030
and about the same up in 2021.

00:31:41.030 --> 00:31:42.810
So is it a good time to buy a house?

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:44.320
Well it's a good time to buy a house

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:47.750
if you already have a house in mind,

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:50.330
you have the down payment,

00:31:50.330 --> 00:31:52.840
you are still employed,

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:54.620
you think you will be employed.

00:31:54.620 --> 00:31:57.720
So if all of those things are in place,

00:31:57.720 --> 00:31:59.330
it's a good time to buy a house.

00:31:59.330 --> 00:32:00.790
It's not a good time to buy a house

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:02.300
if you didn't have all that in place

00:32:02.300 --> 00:32:03.133
and you just think,

00:32:03.133 --> 00:32:05.300
"Wow, maybe it would be a
good time to buy a house."

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:08.460
Because housing prices,
the theory would be

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:10.200
that housing prices will go down.

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:11.950
They might go down a little bit,

00:32:11.950 --> 00:32:14.500
but I said earlier
something that's unusual,

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:16.380
which is there's not a big glut

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:18.640
of available houses on the market,

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:20.760
and that's when they really go down.

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:25.180
So might there be a
reduction in housing prices?

00:32:25.180 --> 00:32:28.420
Yes, because someone may
wanna sell their house

00:32:28.420 --> 00:32:31.060
because they already
wanted to sell their house.

00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:33.770
But I don't think it will be pervasive.

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:37.070
Another question was about
the market, buy or sell now?

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:38.920
Well you wouldn't sell in a down market

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:40.820
if you can help it, that's for sure.

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:44.100
And I listened to Mellody yesterday

00:32:44.100 --> 00:32:46.750
on buying when the market is down.

00:32:46.750 --> 00:32:48.730
I don't give investment advice,

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:50.951
so I don't think I should start now.

00:32:50.951 --> 00:32:53.080
Other than to say,

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:55.800
I still say live within your means.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:58.550
I don't do too much myself
in the middle of a crisis,

00:32:58.550 --> 00:33:00.635
in terms of buying or selling,

00:33:00.635 --> 00:33:03.063
and I guess I wouldn't recommend it.

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:06.368
- Yeah, and I'll just triply underline

00:33:06.368 --> 00:33:08.110
your live within the means.

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:09.070
I grew up in a household,

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:11.416
you know we used to reuse paper towels,

00:33:11.416 --> 00:33:13.510
and I actually only later learned

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:15.510
that wasn't normal behavior.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:18.901
We used to always dry out our
paper towels after using it,

00:33:18.901 --> 00:33:20.380
'cause you know my mom made

00:33:20.380 --> 00:33:23.100
maybe $16,000 a year as a cashier.

00:33:23.100 --> 00:33:25.470
But that lesson, I think if you have

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:28.077
that kind of financial resilience,

00:33:28.077 --> 00:33:33.077
it pays a lot of dividends
well beyond timing the market,

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:36.440
or getting in on some amazing
investment opportunity.

00:33:36.440 --> 00:33:37.750
That's great if that happens,

00:33:37.750 --> 00:33:41.223
but it's really about what you, yeah.

00:33:42.101 --> 00:33:44.430
- I'll tell something I am thinking about,

00:33:44.430 --> 00:33:48.625
which is we have been working very hard

00:33:48.625 --> 00:33:53.625
on the Sustainable Development Goals

00:33:53.800 --> 00:33:55.370
for the last several years.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:58.350
And I don't want other companies,

00:33:58.350 --> 00:34:00.590
or our company, and we won't,

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:02.730
lose sight of the importance of that.

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:06.410
So we're the largest
underwriter of green bonds,

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:08.020
we're a big issuer of green bonds.

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:10.150
And green bonds are like municipal bonds,

00:34:10.150 --> 00:34:11.300
they're kind of boring.

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:13.730
They pay a low interest rate

00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:16.960
but they have very low volatility.

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:18.670
And the idea behind a green bond

00:34:18.670 --> 00:34:22.230
versus a municipal bond is that,

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:25.780
at the end of the duration of the bond

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:30.780
you have to demonstrate that
it did some environmental good.

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:34.390
So just to use a truly prosaic example

00:34:34.390 --> 00:34:36.290
is the City of Los Angeles.

00:34:36.290 --> 00:34:38.530
A bond was issued for them

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:42.770
to change over their
lighting to LED lighting,

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:44.470
and this is a few years ago.

00:34:44.470 --> 00:34:47.120
Now that's, okay, that's
a good thing to do.

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:50.760
But in doing it they
lowered their energy bill

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:54.560
and they were able to pay
the bond off kind of easily,

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:56.663
because actually, in the longer term,

00:34:57.920 --> 00:34:59.770
their electricity bills are lowered,

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:01.114
it's better for the environment.

00:35:01.114 --> 00:35:03.140
And that kind of innovative thinking,

00:35:03.140 --> 00:35:05.240
we don't wanna lose sight
of that in a time like that,

00:35:05.240 --> 00:35:06.610
and that sometimes happens.

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:08.883
But I don't think it will this time.

00:35:11.990 --> 00:35:13.670
It's kind of in the DNA I think

00:35:13.670 --> 00:35:15.860
of Americans to start thinking about this.

00:35:15.860 --> 00:35:17.687
So we're thinking about it

00:35:17.687 --> 00:35:21.450
and not losing sight of how
to help on affordable housing,

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:24.430
or how to help on the environment,

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:26.590
or how to help on education.

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:31.120
Because these things will
be here six months from now,

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:32.390
two years from now,

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:34.470
and there will be a vaccine at some point,

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:36.540
people will be vaccinated,

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:38.820
there will be remedies for this.

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:42.900
And these larger, big,
issues don't go away.

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:45.580
And unless we continue and invest in them

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:46.963
they only will worsen.

00:35:47.970 --> 00:35:49.240
- Yeah, and maybe related to that,

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:50.740
just to finish off on a high note.

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:53.660
Obviously, this is a strange time

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:55.210
for the whole planet right now.

00:35:55.210 --> 00:35:56.500
There's a lot of bad news out there

00:35:56.500 --> 00:35:58.850
on the healthcare front,
on the economic front.

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:01.840
You know dream a little bit,

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:03.720
could there potentially
be some silver linings?

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:06.900
Could there be some things
that we come out of this crisis

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:09.630
maybe a little bit better
than when we went in?

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:11.053
Any ideas on your side?

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:14.680
- Yeah, I have a lot of
thoughts around that.

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:16.793
First of all, I think people are,

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:22.610
you know many people
are working from home.

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:26.090
So the ability to sort of
engage with your family

00:36:26.090 --> 00:36:26.923
in a way that you have,

00:36:26.923 --> 00:36:30.240
and for a long time, is really important.

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:31.786
And also an appreciation for those people

00:36:31.786 --> 00:36:33.720
that are not working at home,

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:36.430
that are on the front
lines in a critical job.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:40.310
I mean the people that we
have in the banking centers,

00:36:40.310 --> 00:36:43.450
whether we're making sure
they have protection,

00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:46.980
and gloves, and masks, and
bringing in their lunch to them,

00:36:46.980 --> 00:36:48.300
and that sort of thing.

00:36:48.300 --> 00:36:49.820
You just start to appreciate

00:36:49.820 --> 00:36:52.370
those people that are on the front line.

00:36:52.370 --> 00:36:54.300
And I think about the medical community

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:55.640
and all they're doing.

00:36:55.640 --> 00:36:58.720
I think sometimes with take
these things for granted,

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:01.240
doctors, nurses, EMTs,

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.767
we don't take them for granted anymore.

00:37:03.767 --> 00:37:06.130
In the same way that you didn't take

